1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,185
A few weeks ago, a founder named Dana Whitfield sat down with four ad variants her

2
00:00:05,185 --> 00:00:10,139
media buyer had just generated for her sock brand, made in an afternoon instead of a

3
00:00:10,139 --> 00:00:11,082
week-long shoot.

4
00:00:11,082 --> 00:00:13,146
She had to choose one to run first.

5
00:00:13,146 --> 00:00:17,981
If that sounds familiar, you've probably already lived a version of it yourself: a

6
00:00:17,981 --> 00:00:22,875
product typed into a generator, and ten minutes later more ad creative than a whole

7
00:00:22,875 --> 00:00:24,173
shoot used to produce.

8
00:00:24,173 --> 00:00:26,472
Here's the part nobody warns you about.

9
00:00:26,472 --> 00:00:27,947
Making the ads got cheap.

10
00:00:27,947 --> 00:00:29,834
Knowing which one to run didn't.

11
00:00:29,834 --> 00:00:33,136
That second problem is what this course exists to solve.

12
00:00:33,136 --> 00:00:37,912
You'll watch it happen to Dana later in this section, then feel it happen to you.

13
00:00:38,462 --> 00:00:40,759
Quick honesty check before we start.

14
00:00:40,759 --> 00:00:44,588
I'm not a career media buyer, and I won't pretend otherwise.

15
00:00:44,588 --> 00:00:49,757
What I bring is the AI-systems side: years building AI products in Silicon Valley

16
00:00:49,757 --> 00:00:54,799
and at major technology companies, including ByteDance, with a Computer Science

17
00:00:54,799 --> 00:00:59,393
degree from NUS, an AI specialisation, and a year at Stanford behind it.

18
00:00:59,393 --> 00:01:04,435
That's exactly the discipline this course hands you: not better taste, a better

19
00:01:04,435 --> 00:01:04,945
machine.

20
00:01:04,945 --> 00:01:10,178
Brief it properly, generate at volume, pre-flight it, test it, and let the account

21
00:01:10,178 --> 00:01:11,263
tell you who won.

22
00:01:11,263 --> 00:01:16,559
Judge this course the same way, on whether that machine actually works for you, not

23
00:01:16,559 --> 00:01:17,261
on the bio.

24
00:01:17,811 --> 00:01:20,961
Here's the concrete change by the end of this course.

25
00:01:20,961 --> 00:01:25,597
Right now, the honest version of how creative gets chosen is "I liked this one

26
00:01:25,597 --> 00:01:25,954
best".

27
00:01:25,954 --> 00:01:30,827
By the end, you'll be running a small, disciplined test with a kill rule, the line

28
00:01:30,827 --> 00:01:35,582
you wrote down in advance that says exactly when to stop paying for a loser, and

29
00:01:35,582 --> 00:01:38,138
reading the outcome instead of guessing it.

30
00:01:38,138 --> 00:01:43,011
You'll spend less on production and more on testing, which is the trade this whole

31
00:01:43,011 --> 00:01:43,784
course makes.

32
00:01:43,784 --> 00:01:48,420
And you'll be able to explain, to a founder or a client, exactly why one ad is

33
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:51,213
getting the budget and three others got killed.

34
00:01:51,763 --> 00:01:54,310
Three commitments about how this is built.

35
00:01:54,310 --> 00:01:55,764
First, you build things.

36
00:01:55,764 --> 00:02:00,554
Eleven downloadable artifacts, and they're the actual documents a real creative

37
00:02:00,554 --> 00:02:04,434
operation runs on: a brief, a testing architecture, a scorecard.

38
00:02:04,434 --> 00:02:07,222
You leave with a system, not a highlight reel.

39
00:02:07,222 --> 00:02:10,314
Second, this is deliberately light on any one tool.

40
00:02:10,314 --> 00:02:15,406
Whatever generator is best this month won't be by the time you finish the course, so

41
00:02:15,406 --> 00:02:19,892
what you're learning is the brief, the pre-flight check, and the kill rule

42
00:02:19,892 --> 00:02:20,741
underneath it.

43
00:02:20,741 --> 00:02:22,135
Those outlive the tool.

44
00:02:22,135 --> 00:02:24,075
Third, I'm leaving the costs in.

45
00:02:24,075 --> 00:02:28,925
This is not a "go viral" course, and it's not a claim that AI replaces judgement

46
00:02:28,925 --> 00:02:30,138
about your customer.

47
00:02:30,138 --> 00:02:34,381
Where a human still has to make the call, I'll tell you exactly where.

48
00:02:34,931 --> 00:02:36,321
One ask before we start.

49
00:02:36,321 --> 00:02:41,071
Three lectures from now, I'm going to hand you a real generated ad set and ask you

50
00:02:41,071 --> 00:02:43,620
to guess the winner before you see any data.

51
00:02:43,620 --> 00:02:44,488
Do it properly.

52
00:02:44,488 --> 00:02:45,763
Write your guess down.

53
00:02:45,763 --> 00:02:50,570
That's the exercise I'd fight you on if you tried to skip it, because what it shows

54
00:02:50,570 --> 00:02:54,856
you about your own judgement is the reason the rest of the course matters.

55
00:02:54,856 --> 00:02:55,667
See you there.
